ce:section.ce:para:
The dilution of olive oil waste residues with tap water (WP) was able to increase both biogas production (Fig. 2a) and CH4 concentration (Table 3). The biogas produced was approximately 13.5L/L with a methane content of 74% v/v. The addition of water to the feed mixture mitigates the acidity of the feed and likely decreases the concentration of polyphenols within the reaction medium. As reported in Table 6
                        , the lag phase (λ) is high, almost 2weeks, and HPT is almost 1 month. These facts demonstrate that without employing pretreatment and only diluting the waste products with tap water the kinetics of the bioreaction is slow, likely because of the presence of complex molecular structures within the reaction environment that inhibit the microorganism activity. Intriguingly, the WP test produced the second highest ESI value (ESI 8.62; Table 7
                        ), though this was due to the low energetic requirements of the pretreatment, as it consists only in the addition of water. In fact the only energy consumption required during this pretreatment is producing enough heat to reach the working temperature. Thus, dilution of waste products with water is one of the most competitive pretreatment options among those tested in the present study.
